From de3b484d9385442ac9501a6d4855693e68873533 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nicole Muzzin <nicks@fuckin.email>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 06:37:54 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] net: cap HTTP/1.1 requests at six per host and queue the rest

hyper's client pool has no per-host connection limit, so a page that fans
out 111 module imports opened 111 sockets at once. Against a server with a
small accept backlog the excess SYNs were dropped and retried with
backoff, and the module graph took ~18 s to load instead of under a
second. Chrome allows six HTTP/1.1 connections per host and queues further
requests.

Each request now takes a slot from a per-(scheme, authority) semaphore
before it is sent and holds it until its response body has been read to
the end or dropped, which is when hyper returns the connection to the
pool. A host that answers over HTTP/2 multiplexes on one connection, so
the first HTTP/2 response closes that host's semaphore and releases every
queued request.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
